window.docContents[8].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior":{"contents":"The linear existence theorems work on prescribed finite time intervals. For\nnonlinear parabolic equations, the length of that interval can itself become a\nquestion: a reaction term can outrun diffusion and force a norm to infinity in\nfinite time, and Keller–Segel aggregation can concentrate mass. The finite-time\nresults include the large-data obstruction for\nnonlinear heat and moment-based\nobstructions for Keller–Segel.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.7. Finite-Time Behavior","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Spectral-and-Analytic-Reconstruction":{"contents":"With the convention ,\nFourier analysis converts a constant-coefficient operator\n into multiplication by its symbol . For the shifted\nelliptic equation\n\n\n\nthe transformed equation is\n\n\n\nPositivity gives the pointwise frequency bound\n. The transformed\nequation and the bound are proved for Schwartz  and , at each\nfrequency.\n\nFor heat and one-dimensional waves, the inverse Fourier formulas\nare identified with the Gaussian convolution and d'Alembert solution.\nThose identities carry the analytic PDE and initial traces back to physical\nspace. One-sided Laplace inversion similarly reconstructs a boundary-driven\ntransport solution from a smooth compactly supported causal signal; the\noperational rule keeps the initial term  visible.\nFor Schwartz initial data in any spatial dimension, we also differentiate\nthe inverse Fourier integral directly. The resulting wave is jointly\n, solves the wave equation, and attains both initial data.\n\nPower-series methods reconstruct a local solution from coefficient recurrences\nand require convergence together with a noncharacteristic initial surface.\nAsymptotic methods ask a different question: how does an integral or profile\nbehave as a parameter becomes large or small? Gaussian identities, Laplace\nconcentration, oscillatory cancellation, and periodic averaging quantify the\ndominant contribution in such limits.\n\nSpline collocation is the finite-dimensional counterpart of these\nexpansions. On a carrier where a nonnegative B-spline family sums to one,\ncoefficient bounds become pointwise bounds. The strict Schoenberg–Whitney\ninequalities give a sufficient condition for sampling to determine the\ncoefficients. We prove the recursion and this sufficient condition, and give the\nexact factorization certificate that a validated backend can use to prove a\ncollocation matrix nonsingular.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.2. Spectral and Analytic Reconstruction","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Spectral-and-Analytic-Reconstruction"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Proof-producing-computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--The-Expression-Representation":{"contents":"To differentiate a formula, the algorithm needs to see how it is assembled:\nwhich operation is outermost, which subexpressions it acts on, and where\nvariables occur. We store that structure as a finite expression tree, called\nVerified.Expr ι, rather than only its value as a real function. Its\nconstructors include integer and rational constants, variables, the four\narithmetic operations, negation, natural powers, real powers a ^ b with an\narbitrary real exponent (Mathlib's Real.rpow), sin, cos, atan,\nasin, acos, exp, log, and sqrt, together with pi and e. An\nenvironment\n\nassigns a real value to every variable, and Expr.eval ρ e interprets the\nsyntax tree as a real number.\n\nThis is called a deep embedding. A shallow representation by Lean functions\n is convenient for stating analytic theorems, but an\nopaque function does not expose its constituent operations to a symbolic\nalgorithm. The expression tree does: differentiation, normalization, and\ninterval evaluation recurse over those operations. We use it because these\nthree computations can then share one representation. The price is a proof\nthat each computation has the intended meaning when the tree is evaluated.\nThe bridge theorems provide that proof and return statements about ordinary\nreal functions, such as the derivatives used in the earlier PDE chapters.\n\nThe index type carries arity, not a new scalar type. For example,\nVerified.Expr (Fin 2) is a scalar expression in two ordered real\ncoordinates. The expression  therefore denotes a map\n. A vector-valued expression is represented by a finite family of\nscalar expressions, one for each output coordinate.\n\nThe #cas command and theorem tactics recognize supported ordinary Lean\nnotation and build the tree internally; a real power is recognized whether it\nis written x ^ (r : ℝ) or Real.rpow x r, while a literal natural exponent\nkeeps the natural-power constructor.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Proof-producing computation","header":"10.4.2. The Expression Representation","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Proof-producing-computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--The-Expression-Representation"}});